  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how water molecules are organized around a central particle, emphasizing distinct hydration layers rather than a uniform liquid environment. A large yellow sphere sits in the middle, surrounded first by a tightly packed shell of blue water molecules labeled as hydration water. Outside this is a second, looser ring of green molecules marked as extended hydration water. Beyond these layers, the surrounding red-and-white molecules represent bulk water, distributed more randomly.\n\nThe overall message is that the presence of the central object perturbs nearby water structure over more than one molecular layer. Water closest to the surface appears more ordered and densely arranged, while water farther away gradually transitions toward the less organized bulk phase. The circular inset in the upper right highlights the tetrahedral-like arrangement typical of bulk water, with dashed lines suggesting hydrogen-bond connectivity among neighboring molecules.\n\nTaken together, the image conveys a spatial gradient in water structure: strong surface-influenced ordering near the particle, weaker but still altered organization in an extended shell, and then ordinary bulk water farther out. It is a conceptual depiction of interfacial hydration and how molecular ordering decays with distance from a surface.",
